cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "interactor-physics: MuJoCo as a sandboxed riscv64 interactor"
abstract: >
  An interactor performs actions on entities: a command in, reply bytes out. This one
  is a riscv64 Linux build of MuJoCo with host bindings for driving it as a libriscv
  sandboxed guest. It was seeded from interactor-gyre's vendored MuJoCo work and moved
  here after that project dropped MuJoCo for Godot's Jolt physics on entity and prop
  contact.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/interactor-physics"
keywords:
  - mujoco
  - physics-simulation
  - riscv
  - sandboxing
references:

  - type: software
    title: "MuJoCo: a physics engine for model-based control"
    authors:
      - name: "Google DeepMind"
    url: "https://github.com/google-deepmind/mujoco"
    notes: >
      Vendored at thirdparty/mujoco, a clean checkout of commit
      b85fdca54f0e0038b804af146a0b4e94199e00d0, tag 3.11.0. Taken from upstream rather
      than from interactor-gyre's git history, which briefly held a corrupted subtree
      merge -- the provenance is stated because the obvious source was the wrong one.

  - type: software
    title: "libriscv: a fast and simple RISC-V sandbox"
    authors:
      - family-names: Haugen
        given-names: Alf-André
    url: "https://github.com/libriscv/libriscv"
    notes: >
      The sandbox MuJoCo is driven as a guest inside. It is what makes this an
      interactor with a boundary rather than a library the caller links.

  - type: software
    title: "interactor-gyre"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/interactor-gyre"
    notes: >
      Where cmake/mujoco.cmake, src/physics/mj_physics.c and the one free-fall test came
      from, ported as-is. That project no longer uses MuJoCo, so this is the surviving
      copy rather than a second one.
